Given the root of a complete binary tree, return the number of the nodes in the tree.
According to Wikipedia, every level, except possibly the last, is completely filled in a complete binary tree, and all nodes in the last level are as far left as possible. It can have between 1 and 2h nodes inclusive at the last level h.
Design an algorithm that runs in less than O(n) time complexity.

/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int val;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode() {}
 *     TreeNode(int val) { this.val = val; }
 *     TreeNode(int val, TreeNode left, TreeNode right) {
 *         this.val = val;
 *         this.left = left;
 *         this.right = right;
 *     }
 * }
 */
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 */

class Solution {
    public int countNodes(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) {
            return 0;
        }

        // Calculate the leftmost height
        int leftHeight = 0;
        TreeNode leftNode = root;
        while (leftNode != null) {
            leftHeight++;
            leftNode = leftNode.left;
        }

        // Calculate the rightmost height
        int rightHeight = 0;
        TreeNode rightNode = root;
        while (rightNode != null) {
            rightHeight++;
            rightNode = rightNode.right;
        }

        // If heights match, the subtree is a perfect binary tree
        if (leftHeight == rightHeight) {
            // (1 << leftHeight) calculates 2^leftHeight
            return (1 << leftHeight) - 1;
        }

        // Otherwise, recursively count nodes in left and right subtrees
        return 1 + countNodes(root.left) + countNodes(root.right);
    }
}


